zshoham@intel.com (Ziv Shoham) writes:
>Schroedinger's Cat was dead or alive when it was inserted to the box. Thus, its
>wave function was one of the eigen functions of the cat and not superposition
>of many functions. However it could die from lack of air. What happens then to
>the wave function?

The Cat was alive when it went into the box. The evolution of its wave function
results after a time in a superposition of live-cat and dead-cat states, which
are (by definition) orthogonal. The experiment is set up so that only the
radioactive decay will kill the cat (oxygen tanks or something are supplied,
or it's just not a long enough experiment to run out of air).

