>Hope this explanation is helpful...

Ummm, please don't spout physics jargon if you don't know what you're doing -
it can be hazardous to your reputation. The original poster meant to say
perturb, because measurement collapses the wave-function (which is a massive
perturbation). The experiment is set up *precisely* so that the cat has as much
chance of being alive as dead when the box is opened; the measurement in this
case consists of simply looking at the cat and seeing if it's breathing or not.
Just before you open the box, the cat is indeed in a superposition of states.
The *paradox* involved is that the cat is itself capable of making observations
(and thus of collapsing the wave function) - but QM predicts that the total
cat+atom+cyanide system's wavefunction will *not* collapse unless observed from
the outside, and we can prove that the numbers from this possibility are
different from the numbers for the cat-collapses-its-own-wavefunction
possibility. Also, in one of the outcomes, the cat is *no longer* an observer
('cuz it's dead) - so you have to consider what the combination of observer
in this state superposed with non-observer in that state does...

